ROCR-Runtime (pinned commit 2b22ab0195cc1461cd9abf3b969e9dd7c10af350,
therock-7.14 / ROCm 7.14.0): restore the GFXIP 7/8 double-mapped AQL ring
buffer ("queue_full_workaround_"), deleted upstream along with the rest of
gfx7/8 support.

STATUS: VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE for what it actually fixes -- the
64-packet `hsa_queue_create` floor. Measured on the gfx803 box 2026-08-28:
max queue size 64 -> 131072 packets, and rocclr's live compute queue
64 -> 16384. That is a real 2048x increase and removes the constraint
`graph-replay-batch-chunk-deadlock.patch` exists to work around.

This patch was written believing it was also the root cause of the silent
dispatch hang (problem 1 in RESOLVED_VRAM_MARGINALITY_INVESTIGATION.md). **That was tested
and is false.** The hang reproduces unchanged with this patch applied, on
attempt 2 of a repeat-until-hung loop, matching the historical baseline
exactly -- and it reproduced with a 16384-packet ring, where the mechanism
this patch addresses (ring exactly full) is unreachable for a sweep that
hung after 3 of 120 tiny cases. The hang is not this. See
RESOLVED_VRAM_MARGINALITY_INVESTIGATION.md problem 1 for the falsification and the
measurements.

Kept and shipped anyway: the missing double map is a genuine bug, this is
the correct fix for it, and it is what the kernel has always expected.

WHY
---
On GFXIP 7/8 the CP cannot accept a doorbell that advances a full queue
length. The hardware write index is masked to the ring size, so
wptr == rptr + size is bit-for-bit indistinguishable from wptr == rptr --
an empty ring. When a producer fills the ring exactly, the packet processor
concludes there is nothing to do, parks, and never drains it again. Nothing
recovers it: the producer is already blocked waiting on a completion signal
for a packet the CP will never fetch, so it never rings another doorbell.

ROCm 6.4.4's ROCR states the mechanism and the fix in its own words
(runtime/hsa-runtime/core/runtime/amd_aql_queue.cpp, AqlQueue's ctor):

    // When queue_full_workaround_ is set to 1, the ring buffer is internally
    // doubled in size. Virtual addresses in the upper half of the ring
    // allocation are mapped to the same set of pages backing the lower half.
    // Values written to the HW doorbell are modulo the doubled size.
    // This allows the HW to accept (doorbell == last_doorbell + queue_size).
    // This workaround is required for GFXIP 7 and GFXIP 8 ASICs.

    queue_full_workaround_ =
        (isa->GetMajorVersion() == 7 || isa->GetMajorVersion() == 8) ? 1 : 0;

Doubling the *virtual* allocation over the same backing store gives the
masked index one more bit than the ring holds, which is what makes the full
case distinguishable from the empty one.

The kernel still implements its entire half of this and still expects
userspace to ask for it. amdgpu_amdkfd_gpuvm.c:

    /* Workaround for AQL queue wraparound bug. Map the same
     * memory twice. That means we only actually allocate half the memory. */
    if ((*mem)->aql_queue)
        size >>= 1;

and kfd_mem_attach() maps the one BO at both `va` and `va + bo_size`
(`for (i = 0; i <= is_aql; i++) { ...; va += bo_size; }`). The trigger is
HsaMemFlags.ui32.AQLQueueMemory -> KFD_IOC_ALLOC_MEM_FLAGS_AQL_QUEUE_MEM.
kfd_queue.c's queue-buffer validation carries the matching assumption:

    /* AQL queues on GFX7 and GFX8 appear twice their actual size */
    expected_queue_size = PAGE_ALIGN(properties->queue_size / 2);

Only the ROCR half went away. In 7.14, MemoryRegion::AllocateDoubleMap
still exists but is commented "Deprecated:" and is referenced by exactly one
file -- core/driver/virtio/amd_kfd_virtio_driver.cpp. The KFD driver path
(core/driver/kfd/amd_kfd_driver.cpp, KfdDriver::AllocateMemory) no longer
translates it, so HsaMemFlags.AQLQueueMemory is never set on any real KFD
allocation, and AqlQueue::AllocRegisteredRingBuffer allocates a plain
single-mapped ring of exactly its packet capacity.

How this was found, and the evidence that it is the right answer
----------------------------------------------------------------
The 64-packet queue-size floor this repo has been working around for months
is this bug announcing itself, not a separate quirk. `hsa_queue_create`
fails on gfx803 for every requested size except 64 packets -- documented in
graph-replay-batch-chunk-deadlock.patch and root-caused there only as far as
"EINVAL from somewhere past set_queue_properties_from_user". It is the
kernel check quoted above: reporting the real size N instead of the doubled
2N makes PAGE_ALIGN(N/2) equal N only when N is exactly one page. Every
larger queue fails the exact-match test in kfd_queue_buffer_get(). One
missing flag explains both the cap and the hang.

It also explains, exactly, the two halves of the experiment recorded in
graph-replay-queue-size-cap.patch: reporting 2N *without* allocating the
mirror let hsa_queue_create succeed at full size and then faulted the GPU
under load ("Memory access fault... Page not present", "HW Exception ...
reason: GPU Hang") -- the CP was told to address a 2N span whose upper half
was never mapped. That patch's kernel companion "fixed" the fault by
forcing the HQD back to N, which also removed the workaround and returned
the box to the hang. Both results are what this bug predicts; neither is
what a size-reporting bug alone would produce.

And it matches the one hard hardware observation on record. The MQD dump
taken during a live graph-replay hang (/sys/kernel/debug/kfd/mqds,
cross-checked against /proc/<pid>/mem) showed cp_hqd_pq_wptr advancing
correctly while cp_hqd_pq_rptr sat frozen on a slot whose AQL header still
read HSA_PACKET_TYPE_INVALID -- a CP that believes it has nothing to fetch,
parked, with a ring software considers full.

What this displaces
-------------------
Two earlier explanations in this repo are wrong and are corrected by their
own patch headers now:

- "Lost EOP interrupt" (blit-kernel-eop-interrupt-retry.patch) cannot be the
  mechanism. BlitKernel::SubmitLinearCopyCommand waits with
  HSA_WAIT_STATE_ACTIVE, and both BusyWaitSignal::WaitRelaxed and
  InterruptSignal::WaitRelaxed treat ACTIVE as a pure spin on signal_.value
  -- hsaKmtWaitOnEvent_Ext is only reached on the passive path. The hung
  process pinned at 99% CPU confirms it never blocked on an event. No
  interrupt is involved; the signal value was never written because the
  packet was never fetched.

- "Missing _mm_sfence() / write-combining" (sdma-doorbell-missing-sfence.
  patch, and this repo's 2026-08-24 addition to
  hsa-agent-rejects-legacy-doorbell.patch) rests on a false premise. The AQL
  ring is GTT/userptr and amdgpu never sets AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_CPU_GTT_USWC
  on the KFD path (amdgpu_amdkfd_gpuvm.c: `alloc_flags = 0` for GTT), so
  ttm caching stays ttm_cached: the CPU mapping is write-back, not
  write-combining, and the PTEs carry AMDGPU_PTE_SNOOPED.
  KFD_IOC_ALLOC_MEM_FLAGS_UNCACHED becomes AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_UNCACHED,
  which gmc_v8 does not read at all (only gmc_v9/10/11/12 do, for PTE
  MTYPE). On write-back memory x86 TSO already orders the packet stores
  ahead of the doorbell store; those SFENCEs are inert, not fixes.

Validation, and one trap worth knowing: this must be built against a kernel
WITHOUT REFERENCE-amdkfd-gfx7-8-queue-size-writeback (that patch forces the
HQD back to the single ring size and cancels the double map), and must never
be combined with graph-replay-queue-size-cap.patch. The latter was hit for
real during validation -- a dev tree still carrying that patch doubled the
reported size twice, and KFD rejected every queue with `expected size 0x2000
not equal to mapping addr ... size 0x1000` in dmesg. That dmesg line means
something is doubling twice.

Depends on: hsa-agent-rejects-legacy-doorbell.patch (applies on top of it;
queue_full_workaround_ multiplies the legacy type-0 doorbell mask that patch
restores).

Supersedes: graph-replay-queue-size-cap.patch and its out-of-tree kernel
companion REFERENCE-amdkfd-gfx7-8-queue-size-writeback.patch. Neither may be
applied alongside this one -- see their headers.

WHAT
----
- memory_region.h: AllocateDoubleMap is live again, not "Deprecated:".
- amd_kfd_driver.cpp: translate AllocateDoubleMap to
  HsaMemFlags.ui32.AQLQueueMemory, as ROCm 6.4.4's KfdDriver::AllocateMemory
  did.
- amd_aql_queue.h: add queue_full_workaround_.
- amd_aql_queue.cpp: set it for GFXIP 7/8; request AllocateDoubleMap for the
  ring; publish the doubled VA span in ring_buf_alloc_bytes_ so CreateQueue/
  UpdateQueue describe the real span to the CP; raise the minimum ring to one
  page and halve the maximum (the alias is not usable capacity); mask the
  legacy type-0 doorbell against the doubled size; and reject the
  device-memory ring path on GFXIP 7/8, which cannot be double-mapped.

